arlessly coach fearlessly  because they know ultimately that we're  getting judged together but  i'll be out in front when i and i've  said this before and and i  you know where did i learn this i don't  know mike but and you see  people do this all the time but i'm very  comfortable leading out in front  and i'm extremely comfortable leading  from behind and i know when it's time to  go backstage and let somebody else have  that spotlight  now if i were to pinpoint specific  moments from the chiefs game  that i would want to go back and and  fix or do over or whatever the richard  higgins fumble that goes into the end  zone and out of the end zone  let me get your thoughts on that rule  i i've always hated it  and you have reason to hate it now  although i know from experience  sometimes a coach who was burned by a  bad rule doesn't want it to go away  until he gets to pay off on the other  side  how do you feel about that rule right  now yeah you know i was part of the  2009 uh nfc championship game  where the saints got the ball first in  overtime drove down kicked the field  goal and the game was over  and i think that was the impetus of  changing the rule where both teams  possess it  you know i had never really given a lot  of thought to that rule  and i just that was the rule and and  that's and we coached it  uh based on that rule because it is uh  it's really impactful when you fumble  i would just tell you it was an  unfortunate play uh  i i trust the league in making decisions  that are best for the game going forward  if that's something they want to  look at i'd support that if they don't  we'll continue to coach it  how we do uh it's it's a tough penalty  because it's such an exciting play  and it's so the guys want to score and  they get down there and they want to  reach the ball for the pylon and think  about  all the plays over the years uh where  that that's an indelible image  of of guys scoring touchdowns and big  games so  i understand the urge that guys have to  want to  do that but that penalty is just or  excuse me that uh  the enforcement of that rule is so  penalizing to the offense that you  really have to coach it  the way we do which is don't stick that  ball out unless it's fourth down but  that's very hard  uh for athletes and guys that are  competitive and want to score touchdowns  see that's the problem and that's where  the league needs to take a step back  and ask are we taking the incentive out  of having a memorable play i know for a  fact one of the reasons they still use  two sticks and 10 yards of chain is for  that two seconds  of the is it a first down or not when  they stretch it out they could do it  digitally but it wouldn't have that  drama  there's drama in the guy reaching the  football for the pylon i guess there's  more drama if we factor in the  possibilities if it comes out of his  hands and goes out of bounds in the end  zone  the other team gets the ball at the 20.  it's just never made sense to me  have you heard from talking to any other  people that there will actually be  reason and belief that they're going to  take a close look at it or is that just  what people say after a situation like  that happens  yeah i haven't heard anything mike you  know we got the owners meeting coming up  towards the end of the month  and the competition committee will get  together and maybe we'll hear some more  coming at it about that but again  i'm of the belief that you just tell us  what the rules are and we'll coach  accordingly  what do you think about the lowering the  helmet rule as it relates to the fact  that  calls and non-calls aren't subject to  replay review because  in that same moment we had that play out  where  replay review you looked at it while  there was lowering the helmet  and we we've had the argument in the  past about defenseless receivers should  those calls be subject to replay review  would you like to see replay expanded  for plays like that that can be so  impactful  if they're missed or if they're called  incorrectly  i think in general i'm of the opinion  less replay  than more i think as as our fans uh  prefer the game to to go and speed up  and not be stopping  every time you know those are really  tough plays mike when it comes to  lowering the helmet because  uh speaking from experience as a former  defensive back and and not a very good  one but  when you when you go to make a tackle  and your targeting may be great and that  player moves at the last split second  and now all of a sudden you have helmet  to helmet contact so it's just  it's really hard i mean you have running  backs that that lower their pads  as they come into the open field and now  their helmets are down so  it's it's very very difficult i think  first and foremost we should be talking  about the health and safety of our  players  uh so i to support anything that that  encourages  that uh aspect of it but uh  in general i'd tell you i would i prefer  less replay than more  speaking of player health and safety  what's your reaction when you see miles  garrett in his jurassic park  tank top playing basketball  my reaction is i think one of those nba  teams should sign him to a 10-day  contract  yeah he's a great athlete uh that  doesn't surprise me  seeing him move around i've he he knows  this i've told him this i've told all of  our players listen  you got to stay in shape you need to to  do some things that get your cardio  going  uh you got to do it safely and he he's  very very well  aware of that but i i do marvel at his  athleticism  so you didn't do like the chiefs did a  couple of years ago when we saw the  video of patrick mahomes playing  basketball when they told him no patrick  no more basketball  you're fine with guys doing within  reasonable limits what they need to do  to stay in shape  i think that's the key within reasonable  limits they know not to go in some  competitive game where they're going to  turn an ankle or catch an elbow or those  type of things but  if they're working on their body which  you know these guys that is their  lifeblood is is their body if they're if  they're looking to get more athletic and  quicker and stronger  i'm going to support that and within  reason  i know that the league has yet to tell  the teams what the salary cap will be  have they told you when they're going to  tell you what the salary cap will be  yeah i think it's got to come sooner  than later you know you got the  franchise tag  the deadline coming up you know to start  a free agency so  i'm in a good position i have much  really smart people that get to worry  about the nitty-gritty of that uh andrew  barry chris cooper  so i get to sit back and and watch those  guys operate  uh right now they're they're in season  i'm off season  well you know one of the realities of  what's coming and there's a wide  expectation that  players will be cut as teams try to get  under the salary cap  free agents may not get the offers  they're looking for it creates for some  teams an  opportunity to maybe get a better value  dollar for dollar by going with  someone from another team versus a guy  that you  have how comfortable are you with that  possibility of maybe  parting ways with a guy you know a guy  you're comfortable with  but there's another player out there  who's quote unquote better  but you could get him given the dynamics  of this crazy market  for a better dollar for dollar value  than the guy you currently have  yeah i think it's a really good question  mike those are the conversations that i  bet are occurring in  all 32 franchises right now and i think  part of it is you know you're team  building you're not just  acquiring assets so you always are  careful uh not to go  too too overboard and and make sure that  you're just  making moves just to make moves and that  that's not andrew's style at all  andrew's got a plan and and together  we've talked about it and you got to be  ready for those type of opportunities  because  to your point they may occur in this  environment  but we won't do anything that's not well  thought out how much does andrew barry  the gm of the team  accept and listen to your input about  players that  that you want personally that you would  like to have on the team for whatever  reason fits the scheme  good leader fresh legs whatever it may  be how much  does what you want get factored into the  decision making yeah we have a great  dialogue mike uh throughout the season  in the offseason now as we're watching  college players  you know he's watched all these guys i'm  catching up to him so he'll tell me  about it  to go watch a player but he'll make sure  not to to lead me on whether he's good  or bad and then i'll circle back with  him and  and we see things very similarly we also  uh you know  last year sat down and hashed out what  we want this team to look like so  i'm extremely comfortable in andrew  picking the players he's really good at  it  uh and and he listens he's a great  listener both to me  to the coaching staff to the personnel  staff so he does a great job of being  collaborative but ultimately  i trust him to make these decisions and  i think organizationally we trust him to  make these decisions  i know when you had a press conference  yesterday you were asked about jj watt  and you prefer not to talk about  individual players but let me ask you  this my niece  huge browns fan and she got all bent out  of shape about the possibility of j.j  watt joining the browns and she was  excited i said look  whether he signs or not the fact that  the browns are in the conversation  in and of itself is huge because  five years ago you're not in the  conversation do you take any  pride do you take any positivity out of  the fact that it came out  that a guy like jj watt is seriously  considering joining your team  no not in taking pride in that uh per se  mike i'd tell you  when it comes to free agency you do want  to be a destination that guys want to go  to  uh and i think that's a multitude of of  reasons and and  whether it's you're talking about the  city the ownership  uh the coaching staff the players that  he's playing with  the scheme that they're playing in all  of that uh is  is comes in total when you're talking  about the desire of  a free agent to go somewhere but we'd  always prefer to be on those  destinations that guys  want to check out how closely will you  evaluate the quarterbacks  entering this year's draft yeah quite a  bit i think  it's important to know as much as you  can  about a bunch of different positions now  i'm  i'd be lying if i told you i'm gonna  know everything there is to know about  all these guys we got  i have uh andrew berry for that but i do  want to know  about the the signal callers because  because it's important to have some  institutional  knowledge of them uh but we also need to  make sure at least i need to make sure  that i'm  you know appropriating my time to to a  bunch of other positions so  it's not like the old days as a  quarterback coach where you know  everything there is to know about just  that one position i need to know  a lot about multiple positions is it  from a  competitive standpoint and an  institutional knowledge standpoint is it  about  knowing what you can about the guy  because depending upon how the dominoes  may fall someday you could have a chance  to get him or  is it we're going to be playing him so  the more we know about him now  the better suited we're going to be to  prepare to face him later it's  definitely  you know this guy if he's not on our  team he's going to be on somebody's team  that you may be playing so you want to  know their strengths and their  weaknesses as best you can  jared goff carson wentz guys who got big  contracts after three years in the nfl  after five years in the nfl both are  going to be traded officially on march  17 unless  in theory the deals fall through how  much of that is a cautionary tale  for teams that you know hypothetically  have a first round quarterback who's got  three years in and may or may not be  getting a new contract  yeah i think you always want to take in  all the information you can and i know  that's fresh  on them on the minds of everybody  because you know as as fans and as  followers of this league you see that  happening but  you know we take in all the information  from many years  of experience doing this to to inform  our decisions and  and try to not make any decisions based  on the immediacy of what's going on  uh right then and there what do you do  now at this stage of the off season as  it relates to  your preparation for next year whether  it's working on the playbook  evaluating personnel what is it you do  when you show up at the facility  until when you go home yeah i think  you kind of cut your day in half mike so  uh  you know for us we start with scheme  evaluation in the morning and i think  that's such a  big part of what we do in this off  season is pulling apart  ourselves and learning about ourselves  and honestly that's  some of the most fun i've had as a coach  are off seasons off-seasons  and the moment that we're in right now  where you sit together together with  your staff  you put on a ton of tape and you watch  it in order and it's amazing  how it's hard in season to see the  forest through the trees  and then in the off season when you put  it all together and there's no  deadline of hey we got a game coming up  on this sunday you can spend time with  it  we learn a ton about ourselves and  that's what we're doing right now  that process will wrap up in the next  couple weeks but  in terms of how do we get better i think  that's one way we need to  we need to uh tinker uh with what we're  doing i'm not saying we have to have  wholesale  changes to what we're doing offensively  defensively special teams but  we absolutely gotta find new ways of  doing things that that can 